
DEVELOPER CONSOLE = https://developer.pesapal.com/integration

Context: TEST / SANDBOX

Step 1: Start ngrok

    Run your Spring Boot app locally (port 9500).

    Open a terminal and start ngrok for your local port:

    ngrok http 9500


    After starting, you’ll see something like:

    Forwarding                    https://abcd1234.ngrok.io -> http://localhost:9500


    Use the HTTPS URL (https://abcd1234.ngrok.io) — Pesapal requires HTTPS for webhooks.

Step 2: Set up your IPN endpoint in Spring Boot

    Make sure your controller endpoint for Pesapal webhooks exists:

    @PostMapping("/api/pesapal/ipn")
    public void handlePesapalIPN(@RequestBody String payload) {
        System.out.println("Received IPN payload: " + payload);
    }

Step 3. To get the access token, use the following
    Method: POST
    Endpoint: https://cybqa.pesapal.com/pesapalv3/api/Auth/RequestToken
    Payload: 
        {
            "consumer_key": "YOUR_CONSUMER_KEY",
            "consumer_secret": "YOUR_SECRET_KEY"
        }

    Response: 
        {
            "token": "*************.*************.*************",
            "expiryDate": "2025-12-15T10:12:23.5213905Z",
            "error": null,
            "status": "200",
            "message": "Request processed successfully"
        }


Step 4.  To Register IPN (Register IPN with Pesapal sandbox) using the PUBLIC URL
    Method: POST
    Endpoint: https://cybqa.pesapal.com/pesapalv3/api/URLSetup/RegisterIPN
    Payload: 
        {
            "url": "https://a1b2c3d4.ngrok.io/api/pesapal/ipn", // The tunneled link eg grok (https) and include the api/pesapal/ipn
            "ipn_notification_type": "POST"
        }


    NB: You must provide the auth token in the headers as Authorization Bearer token

    Response: 
        {
            "url": "https://a1b2c3d4.ngrok.io/api/pesapal/ipn",
            "created_date": "2025-12-15T09:26:29.8984437Z",
            "ipn_id": "*************",
            "notification_type": 1,
            "ipn_notification_type_description": "POST",
            "ipn_status": 1,
            "ipn_status_decription": "Active",
            "status": "200",
            "message": "Request processed successfully"
        }

    Have the IPN ID registered in the application.properties or .yaml and use it in the code ie service implementation


Step 5: Test payment request

    Use Swagger or Postman to call your /api/pesapal/pay endpoint with your test payment data.

    Check console logs from your debugged PesapalServiceImpl. You should see:

    Using IPN ID: *************
    Payment Payload: {...}
    Pesapal Response: {... "redirect_url": "https://cybqa.pesapal.com/..."}


    Pesapal will send POST notifications to your ngrok URL (/api/pesapal/ipn) when payment status updates.



PAYLOADS: http://localhost:9500
1. "/api/pesapal/pay"

{
  "customerId": "123456",
  "amount": 10,
  "currency": "KES",
  "description": "Demo",
  "callbackUrl": "https://*************.ngrok-free.app/api/pesapal/payment-completed"
}

